# Auto-wake proxy — Caddyfile
#
# SUPERSEDED as the deployed artifact by ../../pyinfra/templates/Caddyfile.j2
# (../../pyinfra/deploy_caddy.py, per .scratch/pyinfra-provisioning/spec.md
# and ADR-0008) -- that templated version is what pyinfra actually renders
# onto the Pi. Kept here as the design reference and plugin-research record
# (see README.md); new workload routes go in deploy_caddy.py's WORKLOADS
# list, not this file.
#
# Component: "Auto-wake proxy" (see CONTEXT.md glossary and the parent spec's
# Implementation Decisions bullet, .scratch/homelab-control-system/spec.md).
# Ticket: .scratch/homelab-control-system/issues/05-auto-wake-proxy.md
#
# Runs on the Pi Zero. Reverse-proxies every Docker Compose workload on the
# main server (Immich, AI agents, etc.) and, on any request while the server
# is asleep, sends it a Wake-on-LAN magic packet and holds/retries the
# request until the server answers — transparent to the caller, not an
# error. Per ADR-0005 this is a second, fully independent wake trigger from
# the Control Pi API's deliberate /wake endpoint (ticket 03): nothing in
# this file calls, imports, or otherwise depends on the Control UI or
# Control Pi API, so this path keeps working even if both of those are down
# (see "Independence from the Control UI/API" below).
#
# Plugin required (this is NOT a stock Caddy build — see "Build" below):
#
#   github.com/dulli/caddy-wol — registers the `wake_on_lan` HTTP handler
#   directive used below. Full rationale for choosing this plugin over the
#   alternative found (github.com/Stoufiler/caddy-jellywol), and for
#   researching-but-not-adopting github.com/tailscale/caddy-tailscale for
#   tailnet binding, lives in README.md's "Plugins" section — not repeated
#   here to avoid two copies of the same argument drifting out of sync.
#
# Build (not part of this ticket's scope — pyinfra's future Caddy Concern
# owns how this actually gets built/installed on the Pi, per ADR-0008 and
# .scratch/pyinfra-provisioning/spec.md):
#
#   xcaddy build --with github.com/dulli/caddy-wol
#
# Validated against this exact single-plugin build via `caddy validate`
# and a live `caddy run` smoke test against a deliberately-unreachable
# backend — see services/auto_wake_proxy/README.md for how and what was
# checked, including a control run confirming the file also validates
# with github.com/tailscale/caddy-tailscale added (it isn't required by
# any directive here; only relevant if a future change adopts `bind
# tailscale/<node>` — see README).
{
	# Required by caddy-wol: the wake_on_lan handler must run before the
	# built-in `respond`/`error` handlers it's paired with in handle_errors
	# below, or its directive order in the compiled routes would be wrong.
	order wake_on_lan before respond

	# --- ADR-0004 hard requirement: never bound off-tailnet -------------
	# Every site in this file binds only to this address by default. This
	# mirrors control_pi_api's and control_server_api's `bind_host` (see
	# services/shared/src/control_plane_shared/bind_safety.py and each
	# app's wsgi.py, which default to 127.0.0.1) and is expected to be
	# exposed to the tailnet the same way those are: by an external
	# `tailscale serve` instance in front of Caddy, per CONTEXT.md's
	# description of how the Control UI is served ("by Caddy on the Pi
	# Zero via `tailscale serve`"). Note that a Control-UI-serving Caddy
	# config doesn't exist in this repo yet (no ticket has built it) — this
	# file only adds the workload routes; `default_bind` here just ensures
	# whatever else eventually shares this Caddy instance inherits the
	# same loopback-only default rather than each site needing to repeat
	# it. Override via env var only if this Caddy is bound directly to the
	# Pi's tailnet-interface IP instead of loopback; never set this to a
	# wildcard or off-tailnet address.
	default_bind {$AUTO_WAKE_PROXY_BIND_HOST:127.0.0.1}
}

# --- Reusable auto-wake route -------------------------------------------
#
# One snippet, imported once per workload site block below, so every
# workload gets identical wake/hold/retry behavior without repeating it.
# {args[0]} is the upstream address (host:port) for that workload on the
# main server.
#
# Acceptance-criteria mapping (see the ticket's four checkboxes):
#
#   AC1 "Caddy's WoL plugin is configured ... reverse-proxying every
#        current Docker Compose workload" -> this snippet + the `import
#        auto_wake_route` line in each site block below.
#   AC2 "A request ... while the server is asleep triggers a WoL packet
#        automatically, with no interaction with the Control UI or
#        Control Pi API" -> the `wake_on_lan {$SERVER_MAC_ADDRESS}` line.
#        Nothing in this file references the Control UI's or Control Pi
#        API's origins, ports, or endpoints (grep confirms this — see
#        README's "Independence from the Control UI/API" check).
#   AC3 "The triggering request is held/retried until the server
#        responds, and then succeeds" -> the *second* reverse_proxy call
#        inside handle_errors, with `lb_try_duration 120s`: Caddy keeps
#        retrying the same original request against the same upstream for
#        up to 2 minutes (long enough to cover a typical suspend-to-RAM
#        resume) before giving up, and returns whatever that upstream
#        eventually answers rather than the original 502.
#   AC4 "This path ... functions independently even if the Control
#        UI/Control Pi API are down" -> structurally true by construction:
#        this file contains no upstream, header, or directive referencing
#        either of those services (see README for how this was verified).
(auto_wake_route) {
	# First attempt: short, cheap check of whether the server is already
	# up. lb_try_duration here is intentionally short — this is just
	# "is it awake right now", not the hold/retry window (that's below).
	reverse_proxy {args[0]} {
		lb_try_duration 3s
		lb_try_interval 500ms
	}

	# reverse_proxy above writes a 502 when it can't reach the upstream at
	# all (connection refused/timeout — the suspended-server case) after
	# exhausting the short retry window. This is Caddy's own fabricated
	# error for "no reachable backend", not a real HTTP response — it
	# *does* reach handle_errors. Confirmed empirically (see README) that
	# this stays narrowly scoped to true connection failures: a live
	# upstream that itself answers with a genuine 502 (an app-level error,
	# not "asleep") gets proxied straight through as an ordinary response
	# and does NOT enter this handle_errors block — no spurious WoL packet,
	# no needless hold. That's the general Caddy behavior the docs describe
	# ("certain directives ... which may write a response with an HTTP
	# status classified as an error will not trigger the error routes")
	# working in our favor here, verified with a live backend that
	# returned its own 502 and was passed through unmodified.
	handle_errors {
		@server_asleep expression {err.status_code} == 502
		handle @server_asleep {
			# AC2: the automatic, Control-UI/Control-Pi-API-independent
			# wake trigger. caddy-wol internally throttles repeat packets
			# to at most one per host per 10 minutes, so concurrent
			# requests during the same wake cycle don't flood the network.
			wake_on_lan {$SERVER_MAC_ADDRESS} {$WOL_BROADCAST_ADDRESS:255.255.255.255:9}

			# AC3: hold/retry the *same original request* against the
			# same upstream, now with a much longer window, so the caller
			# gets a real response instead of an error while the server
			# wakes from suspend-to-RAM. 120s is a bound, not a promise —
			# an HTTP request can't be held open forever. If a resume ever
			# takes longer, this attempt gives up and returns a real 502,
			# but the WoL packet has already been sent (and stays throttled
			# for 10 more minutes per host by caddy-wol), so simply
			# reloading catches the now-awake server on the fast path above
			# without re-triggering a second wake.
			reverse_proxy {args[0]} {
				lb_try_duration 120s
				lb_try_interval 1s
			}
		}
	}
}

# --- Workload routes ------------------------------------------------------
#
# EXAMPLE / PLACEHOLDER ROUTES. There are no docker-compose.yml files for
# Immich or "AI agents" anywhere in this repo yet (confirmed by search
# before writing this file) — the parent spec names them only as
# illustrative examples of "every current Docker Compose workload on the
# main server" (spec.md Solution section). The local port numbers below are
# placeholders to be replaced with real values once those workload stacks
# exist; do not treat them as verified facts about a real deployment.
#
# {$MAIN_SERVER_HOST} is a single placeholder for the main server's
# tailnet address/hostname (e.g. a MagicDNS name like
# main-server.<tailnet>.ts.net) — every workload lives on the same
# physical server, so they share this one placeholder and differ only by
# upstream port.
#
# Site addresses here are bare `:port` (plain HTTP on the loopback address
# from default_bind above) rather than a public-looking hostname. That's
# deliberate: a hostname-shaped site address would make Caddy try to
# provision its own ACME/TLS certificate for it, which is wrong for this
# topology — tailnet-facing HTTPS and each workload's public hostname are
# `tailscale serve`'s job (one `tailscale serve` port mapping per route
# below), exactly as CONTEXT.md documents for the Control UI. Wiring the
# actual `tailscale serve` mappings for these ports is a pyinfra Caddy
# Concern task, out of this ticket's scope (see the Build note above).

# Immich (self-hosted photo/video management). Immich's own docker-compose
# distribution maps its web UI to host port 2283 by default
# (https://docs.immich.app/overview/quick-start/) — used here as a
# plausible illustrative upstream port, not a value verified against this
# repo's (nonexistent) deployment. :8443 (this Caddy site's own listening
# port, to be given a `tailscale serve` mapping) is likewise illustrative.
:{$IMMICH_ROUTE_PORT:8443} {
	import auto_wake_route {$MAIN_SERVER_HOST}:2283
}

# "AI agents" workload stack named in the parent spec with no further
# detail and no docker-compose file in this repo — both the upstream port
# (8080) and this site's own listening port (8444) are generic,
# clearly-invented placeholders only, to be replaced once that stack is
# actually designed and built.
:{$AGENTS_ROUTE_PORT:8444} {
	import auto_wake_route {$MAIN_SERVER_HOST}:8080
}

# Add one site block per additional Docker Compose workload as they're
# built, each a single `import auto_wake_route <host>:<port>` line plus a
# corresponding `tailscale serve` mapping for its listening port. Per
# ADR-0008, once pyinfra's Caddy Concern exists, this whole file (Control
# UI static-file serving plus these workload routes) becomes the single
# declarative source of truth templated onto the Pi — no hand-edited copy
# on the device itself.
